fr:tree.fr:mainmatter:
Let’s say we want to perform A[I_X,J_Y]\cdot _J B[J_Y,K]\to  C[I_X,K] on TPU v5e 4x4. What communication do we perform? How much time is spent on communication vs. computation?

What about A[I_X,J]\cdot _J B[J_X,K_Y]\to  C[I_X,K_Y]? This is the most standard setting for training where we combine data, tensor, and ZeRO sharding.

What about A[I_X,J]\cdot _J B[J,K_Y]\to  C[I_X,K_Y]? This is standard for inference, where we do pure tensor parallelism.






  Solution


Since this is a matmul over the sharded axis, we need to perform a reduction at some point. After performing the matmul, we should have C[I_X, K]\{U_Y\}. Therefore, we need to do an AllReduce over Y. This is then a ReduceScatter and an AllGather. For computation, each chip does the partial sum, which takes \frac {2IJK}{|X||Y|} FLOPs. For communication, the AllReduce is 2 \times   an AllGather. We are still sharded over X, so the total bytes is N = \frac {2IK}{|X|}. We do not have bi-directional because the v5e is a 2d topology and 4 \times  4 is not full in either axis. Thus, the communication is
\frac {2\cdot  N}{W_{\text {ICI}}} = \frac {4IK}{4.5e10\cdot  |X|}

To see where we are bound, the v5e can do 1.97e 14 bf16 FLOPs/s. Therefore, we are compute bound when

  \begin {align*}
  \frac {2IJK}{|X|\cdot  |Y|\cdot  1.97e 14} > \frac {4IK}{4.5e10\cdot  |X|}
\iff  
  J > \frac {8 \cdot  1.97e 14}{4.5e10}
  .\end {align*}


Thus, we are almost surely communication bound because it is determined by the contracting dimension, which is d_{\text {model}} in practice.


Remark. 
No solutions available. Prayers up this right...



This is Case 1 with an additional sharding over the contracting dim in B. I believe this means when we do the matmul, we are gonna end up with scattered partial sums i.e. unlike in the ReduceScatter case, where we have partial sums the same dimension as C, the partial sums will now be \frac {I}{|X|} \times  \frac {K}{|Y|}. Another ReduceScatter would cause this smaller matrix to be further divided and scattered, so it is not what we want.

Instead, we should just perform \text {AllGather}_X(B[J_X, K_Y]). Then, this will directly reduce to Case 1 and we will need no further communication. We only need to send N = \frac {2JK}{|Y|} bytes. Once again, we do not have bi-directional bandwidth. Thus, the communication is
\frac {N}{W_{\text {ICI}}} = \frac {4JK}{4.5e10\cdot  |Y|}

The FLOPs per-device are still \frac {2IJK}{|X|\cdot  |Y|}. To see where we are bound, the v5e can do 1.97e 14 bf16 FLOPs/s. Therefore, we are compute bound when

  \begin {align*}
  \frac {2IJK}{|X|\cdot  |Y|\cdot  1.97e 14} > \frac {2JK}{4.5e10\cdot  |Y|}
\iff  
  I > \frac {4 \cdot  1.97e 14}{4.5e10}
  .\end {align*}


Thus, we have a smaller bound than the previous part but are now dependent on I instead of J. This is likely in practice because I corresponds to sequence length, which can get very large.

This is pure case 1 with no communication, so we instead need to worry about memory instead of communication overhead.

